1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,610
This boat costs about
two thousand dollars.

2
00:00:02,020 --> 00:00:03,630
This one costs thirteen billion.

3
00:00:04,020 --> 00:00:05,610
Between them is a whole ladder of ships.

4
00:00:05,660 --> 00:00:08,590
And some of the prices
don't make sense at all.

5
00:00:08,820 --> 00:00:11,590
One ship on this list is less
than half the length of a big

6
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:14,550
container ship, but it costs
more than two of them together.

7
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:18,030
Let's climb every level of ship,
from cheapest to most expensive.

8
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:19,710
Let's start at the bottom.

9
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:22,050
A fourteen-foot aluminium fishing boat.

10
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:25,910
A basic new one costs about two
thousand dollars, for the hull alone.

11
00:00:26,220 --> 00:00:28,010
That's less than many phones and laptops.

12
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:31,290
Add a small motor and a
trailer, and most people pay

13
00:00:31,340 --> 00:00:33,830
somewhere between five and
sixteen thousand dollars.

14
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:35,300
Why so cheap?

15
00:00:35,480 --> 00:00:38,490
It's a simple shape,
made of thin aluminium,

16
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:42,170
and factories build thousands
of them exactly the same way.

17
00:00:42,220 --> 00:00:46,170
Buy one used, and it's often thirty
to fifty percent cheaper again.

18
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:49,230
Now, remember the six-hundred-horsepower

19
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:51,210
outboard from our engine video?

20
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:53,650
Mercury's V12 Verado cost about

21
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:55,450
seventy-seven thousand dollars at launch.

22
00:00:55,500 --> 00:00:58,450
And fast boats hang up to
four of them on the back.

23
00:00:58,740 --> 00:01:01,090
That's more than three hundred
thousand dollars of engines,

24
00:01:01,140 --> 00:01:03,310
before you've even bought the boat.

25
00:01:03,540 --> 00:01:06,670
So on the water, expensive
starts very quickly.

26
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:09,750
Next, a working boat: the harbour tug.

27
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:13,150
Shipyards rarely publish new
prices, but here's a real one.

28
00:01:13,340 --> 00:01:17,170
Damen, one of the biggest tug builders
in the world, listed a used harbour tug,

29
00:01:17,220 --> 00:01:21,410
built in 2015, for between
four and six million euros.

30
00:01:21,720 --> 00:01:23,790
That's for a boat about
twenty-eight metres long.

31
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:25,090
Second-hand.

32
00:01:25,180 --> 00:01:26,330
Where does the money go?

33
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:27,910
A tug is mostly engine.

34
00:01:28,180 --> 00:01:31,630
Two big diesels, propellers that
can spin all the way around,

35
00:01:31,680 --> 00:01:35,350
and a hull strong enough to push
ships thousands of times its weight.

36
00:01:35,540 --> 00:01:37,770
It's a tiny boat built like a bulldozer.

37
00:01:38,020 --> 00:01:40,610
Now we jump to the biggest
cargo ships on Earth.

38
00:01:40,940 --> 00:01:44,570
Ultra-large container ships,
almost four hundred metres long,

39
00:01:44,620 --> 00:01:46,970
carrying more than twenty
thousand containers.

40
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:49,670
According to the shipping
analysts at Clarksons,

41
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:52,950
a new one costs around two
hundred and forty million dollars.

42
00:01:53,060 --> 00:01:54,530
That sounds huge.

43
00:01:54,820 --> 00:01:56,910
But for its size, it's actually one of the

44
00:01:56,960 --> 00:01:59,290
cheapest ships per metre
on this whole list.

45
00:01:59,480 --> 00:02:00,830
That's because a container ship is

46
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:03,390
basically a giant steel
box with an engine.

47
00:02:03,640 --> 00:02:05,230
Shipyards build them in series,

48
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:08,550
often a whole batch of the
same design, one after another.

49
00:02:08,840 --> 00:02:11,830
Split the price across twenty-four
thousand container slots,

50
00:02:11,880 --> 00:02:14,710
and each slot costs about
ten thousand dollars.

51
00:02:16,220 --> 00:02:17,850
Here's the strange one.

52
00:02:18,120 --> 00:02:21,530
An LNG carrier is a lot shorter
than the biggest container ships.

53
00:02:21,580 --> 00:02:24,280
But it costs about the same: around two

54
00:02:24,330 --> 00:02:26,260
hundred and forty-five million dollars.

55
00:02:26,420 --> 00:02:27,420
Why?

56
00:02:27,480 --> 00:02:29,290
Because it carries
natural gas as a liquid,

57
00:02:29,340 --> 00:02:32,670
at minus one hundred and
sixty-two degrees Celsius.

58
00:02:33,180 --> 00:02:35,230
The tanks are built like
giant thermos flasks,

59
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:37,190
with layers of special insulation.

60
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:40,470
One small leak of that cold
liquid can crack normal steel.

61
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:42,830
And even with all that insulation,

62
00:02:43,080 --> 00:02:46,230
a little of the gas still warms
up and boils off during the trip.

63
00:02:46,440 --> 00:02:50,070
Many LNG carriers simply burn it
as fuel for their own engines.

64
00:02:50,120 --> 00:02:52,090
So you're not paying for size.

65
00:02:52,420 --> 00:02:54,130
You're paying for extreme cold.

66
00:02:54,440 --> 00:02:57,010
Remember the ship that costs
more than two container ships?

67
00:02:57,420 --> 00:02:58,230
This is it.

68
00:02:58,280 --> 00:03:00,650
Azzam is the longest private
motor yacht in the world,

69
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:02,330
at one hundred and eighty metres.

70
00:03:02,740 --> 00:03:06,030
It was built in Germany by
Lürssen, delivered in 2013,

71
00:03:06,420 --> 00:03:10,130
and it can reach thirty-two knots,
very fast for a ship that size.

72
00:03:10,460 --> 00:03:12,530
Its price was never officially published,

73
00:03:12,620 --> 00:03:16,300
but it's widely reported at around
six hundred million dollars.

74
00:03:16,540 --> 00:03:18,990
That's more than two of the
biggest container ships,

75
00:03:19,040 --> 00:03:21,490
for a boat less than half as long.

76
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:23,990
Per metre, a giant container ship costs

77
00:03:24,040 --> 00:03:26,222
about six hundred thousand dollars.

78
00:03:26,272 --> 00:03:28,470
Azzam costs more than three million.

79
00:03:28,540 --> 00:03:31,050
Five times as much, for every metre.

80
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:34,730
Now the biggest cruise ship in
the world, Icon of the Seas.

81
00:03:34,780 --> 00:03:38,270
Steel cutting started in June
2021, and it was delivered at

82
00:03:38,320 --> 00:03:42,430
the end of 2023, after just
over nine hundred days of work.

83
00:03:42,580 --> 00:03:45,090
Royal Caribbean never
published an official price,

84
00:03:45,180 --> 00:03:47,740
but it's reported at
around two billion dollars.

85
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:51,410
For that price, you get
a floating city: pools,

86
00:03:51,540 --> 00:03:55,330
water slides, restaurants, and
room for thousands of guests.

87
00:03:56,160 --> 00:03:58,690
Then there are ships
most people never see.

88
00:03:58,980 --> 00:04:01,150
An American Virginia-class
attack submarine.

89
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:02,430
According to the U.S.

90
00:04:02,480 --> 00:04:04,970
Congressional Research
Service, each new one now

91
00:04:05,020 --> 00:04:08,200
costs around four and a half
to five billion dollars.

92
00:04:08,360 --> 00:04:10,590
That's more than two Icon of the Seas,

93
00:04:10,640 --> 00:04:13,150
for a ship that spends its
life hidden underwater.

94
00:04:13,460 --> 00:04:15,270
Part of that price is its nuclear reactor.

95
00:04:15,320 --> 00:04:18,770
It's designed to run for the
whole life of the submarine,

96
00:04:18,960 --> 00:04:22,230
more than thirty years,
without ever being refuelled.

97
00:04:22,360 --> 00:04:25,070
And at the top: USS Gerald R. Ford,

98
00:04:25,260 --> 00:04:27,450
the most expensive warship ever built.

99
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:30,670
Its construction cost is about
thirteen billion dollars.

100
00:04:30,780 --> 00:04:34,310
On top of that, billions more were
spent designing the new class.

101
00:04:34,600 --> 00:04:37,490
It's three hundred and
thirty-seven metres long,

102
00:04:37,700 --> 00:04:40,870
weighs about one hundred thousand
tonnes, and carries more than

103
00:04:40,920 --> 00:04:44,890
seventy-five aircraft and around
four and a half thousand people.

104
00:04:45,200 --> 00:04:48,130
Divide the price by its weight,
and every single kilogram

105
00:04:48,180 --> 00:04:50,550
of this ship cost about one
hundred and thirty dollars.

106
00:04:51,580 --> 00:04:54,010
For the price of this one
ship, you could buy more

107
00:04:54,060 --> 00:04:56,630
than fifty of the biggest
container ships in the world.

108
00:04:57,140 --> 00:04:59,330
So from a two-thousand-dollar
fishing boat to a

109
00:04:59,380 --> 00:05:03,290
thirteen-billion-dollar carrier,
ships cost what their job demands:

110
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:07,210
size is cheap, but cold,
luxury, and war are expensive.

111
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:09,390
And buying a ship is only the start.

112
00:05:09,660 --> 00:05:12,250
If you want to know what
it costs just to hire one,

113
00:05:12,380 --> 00:05:13,610
watch this video next.
